<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Visby</h1><p class="st-meta">1161 CE – 1361 CE (approx.) · capital Visby</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Walled Hanseatic town on Gotland, one of the great trading centres of the medieval Baltic, whose walls and churches still stand.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Visby grew on Gotland's west coast as a harbour of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gotland">Gotland</a> farmers' republic. In 1161 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> confirmed the rights of German merchants trading on Gotland, and German traders settled alongside Gotlanders, making Visby the base of the Community of Gotland Merchants who traded with Novgorod.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The town was run by a council of German and Gotlandic merchants and acted with growing independence from the rural island. Around 1300 it built a 3.4-kilometre stone wall, and it filled with stone warehouses and more than a dozen churches. Its maritime law influenced other Baltic ports, and it was a leading member of the early <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Conflict with the rural Gotlanders led to civil war in 1288. In 1361 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-iv-of-denmark">Valdemar IV of Denmark</a> took Visby after defeating the farmers outside its walls, and the town surrendered. Its importance declined as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> rose. Visby is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/visby.jpg" alt="Visby" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: L.G.foto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0522Visby_domkyrka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1161 CE – 1361 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Visby</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Old Gutnish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hanseatic city of Visby, Wisby</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1261&amp;focus=visby" aria-label="Visby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5072 227 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Visby lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5392 483L5237 584M5392 483L5381 440M5392 483L5274 535"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 584h0M5381 440h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5392 483h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5392 483h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1261&amp;focus=visby">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

